<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hwaseong Fortress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1794–1796</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hwaseong Fortress is a walled fortress surrounding the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwon">Suwon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, built from 1794 to 1796 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">King Jeongjo</a> near the relocated tomb of his father, Crown Prince Sado. Its walls run for about 5.7 kilometres with gates, bastions and towers, and its builders used cranes and pulleys designed with the help of the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeong-yak-yong">Jeong Yak-yong</a>. Restored from detailed records of its construction, it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hwaseong-fortress.jpg" alt="Hwaseong Fortress" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lance Vanlewen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hwaseong_Fortress,_Suwon,_Gyeonggi-do,_Republic_of_Korea_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
